## Research Findings
The questionnaire was completed by 14 participants representing different categories of university users. 

The findings provide valuable insights into users' experiences with existing queue management processes and their expectations for a Virtual Queue Management Mobile Application.

1. Participant Demographics
The majority of respondents were students (64.3%), followed by non-teaching staff (21.4%), while teaching staff and visitors each accounted for 7.1% of the participants. Since students make up the largest user group, the findings strongly reflect the experiences of the application's primary target users.
Finding: Students are the primary users of university service points and are therefore the group most affected by long queues.

2. Frequency of Visiting University Service Points
Participants were asked how often they visit university service points such as the Registry, Finance Office, Clinic, and Accommodation Office.
The responses showed that:
28.6% visit service points daily. 
28.6% visit 2–3 times per week. 
28.6% visit rarely. 
14.3% visit occasionally (monthly). 
Finding: More than half of the respondents use university service points regularly, indicating that an efficient queue management system would benefit many users.

3. Service Points Where Users Experience Queues
Participants selected the service points where they had experienced queues.
The Finance Office recorded the highest number of responses (64.3%), followed by the Food's Mess (57.1%) and the Accommodation Office (35.7%). The Registry, Clinic, and Welfare Office each received 28.6%, while the Sports and Games Office had the lowest response (7.1%).
Finding: Queueing is experienced across multiple university service points, with the Finance Office and Food's Mess being the busiest locations.

4. Experience with Long Waiting Times
Participants were asked how often they experience long queues at university service points.
The results showed that:
42.9% experience long queues occasionally. 
28.6% experience them frequently. 
28.6% experience them very rarely. 
0% selected Never. 
Finding: Every participant had experienced long queues at least once, confirming that waiting times are a common issue within the university.

5. Preferred Method of Joining a Queue
Participants were asked how they would prefer to join and track a queue.
The results indicated that:
71.4% preferred joining a queue remotely through a mobile application. 
21.4% preferred taking a physical ticket and waiting on-site. 
7.1% had no preference. 
Finding: There is strong support for replacing traditional physical queues with a virtual queue management system.

6. Willingness to Use the Proposed Mobile Application
Participants rated how likely they would be to use a mobile application that allows them to join a virtual queue, monitor their position, and receive notifications before their turn.
The responses produced an average rating of 4.0 out of 5. Most participants selected ratings of 4 or 5, indicating a high level of acceptance.
Finding: Users are generally willing to adopt a virtual queue management application if it provides convenient queue tracking and timely notifications.

Common Themes
The analysis of the responses revealed several recurring themes:
Long queues remain a common challenge at university service points. 
Students prefer solutions that allow them to continue with other activities instead of waiting in line. 
Users value transparency and want to know their queue position and estimated waiting time. 
Mobile notifications are considered an important feature for preventing missed turns. 
There is strong acceptance of a virtual queue management system among the participants. 

Key Insights
The findings indicate that the proposed Virtual Queue Management Mobile Application should prioritize:
Remote queue registration. 
Real-time queue position tracking. 
Estimated waiting times. 
Push notifications before a user's turn. 
Support for multiple university service points. 
A simple, intuitive, and accessible user interface. 

Surprising Findings
Although the application is primarily intended for students, respondents from different user groups—including teaching staff, non-teaching staff, and visitors—also expressed interest in a virtual queue management system. Additionally, the Food's Mess emerged as one of the busiest locations, suggesting that virtual queue management could also be beneficial beyond administrative offices.

This research confirms that physical queueing is a significant challenge within the university. The strong preference for mobile-based queue management and the high willingness to adopt the proposed application provide clear evidence that a Virtual Queue Management Mobile Application would address real user needs while improving convenience, efficiency, and the overall user experience.